A||||7||82-T/A||Has anyone stripped their WHEELS??||||5||
Z||000000||82-T/A||12-06-2000||09:35 AM||||I'm just curious, I know that on Jason's Michigan Fiero Web site, he has directions to strip a Fiero's Wheels down to the metal, but it only shows it done for the 15s.<P>Here is what I was thinking, then if you know, tell me if anyone has done this.<P>I was hoping to completely strip the damaged acrylic off my 14" Hi-tech wheels on my 84 Fiero. I was then going to polish them, and basically leave them completely unpainted giving it almost that chrome plated look.<P>I'm not really sure how it would look, or if it would be too tacky. Does anyone know of anyone whos done this or possibly have pictures?<P>Thanks!<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||12-06-2000||10:19 AM||||This past spring I bought a set of 15" GT wheels and I stripped them down completely and refinished them. Go to the below archived thread for procedure and pics.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20001206-1-002367.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000002/HTML/20001206-1-002367.html</A> <P>I have seen 14" ones stripped and left bare, They look good. To get the chrome-like finish on the 14" face requires lots of sanding to remove the machining marks.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECO5||12-06-2000||02:07 PM||||Or, you could paint them like I did:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002946.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002946.html</A> ||||reg||14
Z||000003||wahoo4u||12-06-2000||02:47 PM||||i took a set of 14's striped the clearcoat and paint off of them and then repainted them with a silver finish and reclearcoated.  we just took some aircraft paint stripper sprayed it on and brushed it into the smaller areas let sit for a while and then power washed it of.  When we reapplyed the clearcoat, it gave the silver finish a crackled look.  Turned out pretty good i thought.  the were the brightest 14 rims that i have seen with out being aftermarket.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jmw||12-06-2000||05:55 PM||||I'm still working on the tech wheels on my '84. I used regular 'gel' paint stripper. It doesn't run (as much). You 'paint' it on there and let it sit for a while. The clearcoat peels right off. Only problem is, it takes several applications to get it all off (the outer edges are easiest, 'spokes' are the hardest). It's also taken off a little bit of the dark gray paint on the inside edges of the 'spokes' but I'm just gonna touch that up later. My next step is to sand with 600 grit wet-dry, per Forum instructions, then use Mother's aluminum polish, then re-apply clearcoat (if I ever get that far). I want the 'original' look. <P>I will say this - it is very time consuming and tedious. I've heard of places that will strip them (I think they dip them) for like $25 each. If there's a place in your area that'll do that, I highly recommend it.<P>Good luck!||||reg||7
Z||000005||Fformula88||12-06-2000||07:00 PM||||I have done two sets of wheels... a set of 14" Fiero Tri-tech and a set of 14" rims off of a 91 Ford Probe.  Basically, I used mild sanding disks to sand the factory coating off the wheels.  Then I went to Home Depot and got a bunch of different metal polish grades and some polishing disks.  By using the grades, it was a lot quicker to remove the sanding marks and corrosion pits than just using polish.  As a last step I used Mothers polish to brighten them up!  To protect my hard (tedious and time consuming) work, I bought some clear engine paint and gave them a few coats.  This worked well, as it brought out the shine even more (like the clear coat on the cars finish!).  Plus it protected them from moisture and contaminents that could dull them.  Every spring I woulk re-spray a coat of clear on them to cover up and chips from stones and stuff!  <P>I have some detailed explanations and step-by-step instructions on my webpage.  Feel free to check it out!  Good luck!<P> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/wheels.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/wheels.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
